A||||16||fiero56||synthetic blend?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||11-11-2000||10:11 PM||||Wondering what the general feeling is about conventional/synthetic blend oils? Any better than conventional?<P>I am using Valvoline durablend in the SE, and wondering if the blend offers any advantage over conventional. The Formula will stay with a strict diet of conventional 10w40, until it needs 20w50 or I replace the engine. Oil in the Formula is overdue, and I have few plans to change it. I hate that engine. Why won't it die?! <P><BR>Any feelings on Mobil1? I have heard about quality control issues from a person, wondering if anyone else cares to comment.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||11-12-2000||01:00 AM||||Synthetic oil is good stuff if it is used in the engine form the beginning. It doesn't make sense to use it in a high mileage engine because the wear has already happened and the use of synthetic oil in a high mileage engine can cause oil leaks. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||11-12-2000||07:40 AM||||Hmmmm ... IMHO, even in a higher mileage motor, switching to synthetic is a benefit.  Wear continues to occur even on motors with 100,000+ miles.<P>Besides, synthetic provides more benefits than simply reduction in wear.  It also breaks down less easily than conventional and it has a better flow rate in colder temperatures.<P>Whether or not it's worth three times that of conventional is a different story.  I read an article once (I think in one of the car magazines) that stated motor oil manufacturers were beginning to use similar "synthetic" ingrediants in their conventional oils.<P>This topic has the potential of becoming bigger than the "Those Tricky Buccaneers", "Spark Plugs", "Clutch Problems Uncovered" and "Pine Tree Air Fresheners" topics!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||11-12-2000||10:45 AM||||I have always used the part synthetic blend. I have heard that it is the best for most engines. I am going to change the oil here in the next week or so, should I change to full synthetic? I hope this doesnt become the "Champion Spark Plug" revisited thread.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">    <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||11-12-2000||10:53 AM||||Personally I have been using Mobil-1 in all my cars for the last 15 years or so and have been extremely happy with it.  I have switched the Fieros over to Mobil-1 when I bought them.  I have always changed oil and filter at 5,000 mile intervals with Mobil-1, and have usually used AC filters.<P>In my experience, if the car is not leaking oil, then Mobil-1 will not cause it to leak.  If you have bad seals or engine problem, synthetic oil is no cure all.  I do believe it provides better lubrication and longer engine life.  My 85 SE 4cyl with 118K nomally uses about 1 qt of oil during the 5K oil interval.  I normally do not add any oil between changes!  I started using Mobil-1 in this car at about 78K when I bought it.<P>I used to have a Chevy Astro minivan that I switched to MObil-1 at 30K.  I ran this vehicle on a 10,000 mile oil/filter change interval for over 200K with no engine problems.  I donated the van to a church group with 250K on it!<P>I personally highly recommend MObil-1.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-12-2000||11:46 AM||||I also use Mobil-1, but the 50/50 blend. Not to start anything like the Spark Plug thread, but stay away from Pennzoil (sp?) I have had nothing but trouble with it. When the engine gets hot or somthing I look at the oil cap and dipstick and there is tan gunky stuff on it. That cant be good. I talked to mechanics and they said that was normal with that brand. I changed oil with a differnt brand and had no further trouble. Any one else had trouble with Penz?||||reg||1
Z||000006||zeusrekning||11-12-2000||07:21 PM||||My coupe has 224,917 right now and i put amsoil in 2 weeks ago. When i checked it yesterday it was still clear. Like i just put it in. I also used a mobil 1 filter||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||11-13-2000||09:03 PM||||I use Mobil 1 in everything and I'd do it to a high mileage engine in a second. Leaking oil is a different problem.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000008||RossT||11-14-2000||01:10 AM||||I use to use amsoil for many years, but their last price increase was the end of the line for me. I now buy mobil 1 at wallmart for $20.80 a 6 pack. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||tgowens||11-14-2000||02:11 AM||||We use Red Line synthetic at the shop. The use of synthetics will not cause any more of an oil leak than any other oil. The main benefit is longer life of the oil before the viscosity breaks down (15,000 between changes) however we still reccomend changing the filter every 5,000 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Smokey||11-15-2000||03:29 AM||||Synthetic oil can or can not be bad for an engine with high milage. It depends. One thing that synthetic provides and regular oil doesn't is very good cleaning additives. we all know that on high milage engines a "gunk" starts to build up inside after a long period of time. This "gunk" or whatever u would like to call it is in some cases actually keeping your motor nice and quiet. Now we all know that synthetic oil flows thru an engine better. The reason for this is....picture the size of a BB, a golf ball, and a softball, this is what regular oil would look like underneath a microscope. While on the other hand, in synthetic oil they would all be the size of the BB. This alows the oil to flow into areas it harder for conventional oil to get to. Combine that w/ the really good cleaning additives and it could present a problem. I've seen cars that came into work and their owners wanted synthetic oil in it for the first time while it had high milage. They'd come back a little while later just a knockin and tappin away. The sythetic oil would actually take the clean all the gunk out of it that was keeping the engine quiet. Where does it go? Right in the oil pan, putting more stress on the engine. Now this doesn't always happen. It depends on how clean the engine was kept inside through-out it's life. But hey, your car, your decision||||reg||1
Z||000011||wkayl||11-15-2000||08:38 AM||||I just got a new Chev k-1500 4x4 and now have 250 miles on it (see "my new truck" in O/T).  I have always used Quaker State (10-40 in summer and 10-30 in winter) in all my cars with no problems.  My old Truck had 172,000 miles when I parked it in a ditch and never had to add oil between changes.  I like to change oil at 3,000 to 4,000 miles. My Fiero has 92,000 with the same results.  I am going to use Quaker State in it unless I see a convincing argument in here ||||reg||1
Z||000012||zeusrekning||11-15-2000||04:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Smokey:<BR><B>This "gunk" or whatever u would like to call it is in some cases actually keeping your motor nice and quiet. I've seen cars that came into work and their owners wanted synthetic oil in it for the first time while it had high milage. They'd come back a little while later just a knockin and tappin away.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OH $hit... I think i have a rod knocking. No, i'm not kidding. I put amsoil in then come and tell everyone that it is great and still clear and see what happens. I have all ways had a little noise.(thought it may be timing gears(2.5) but now i hear a knocking sound.)) Hope not how can i tell what the noise is????<BR>F$%^<BR>$h!t<BR>D2#%<BR>H&11 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  eek!  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  <P>||||reg||8
Z||000013||rogergarrison||11-15-2000||05:03 PM||||i used to use Valvoline durablend. I run my car pretty hard, lots of 6000 rpm shifts, hi speed hiway cruising. It used a quart of oil about every 1500 miles. I changed to Castrol Full Synthetic and now it never needs any added between changes, and I change it about every 10,000 miles. ive put nearly 40k in last 2 years with no engine mechanical problems.<BR>||||reg||1
